STEP 1 (feasibility gate): FAILS. No faithful cheap map exists from a DNS orbit
snapshot (features.py's U) into the space P is defined on (polar_spectrum.py's
ker(Cg) quotient of the corner solver's state z). Below is exactly what such a map
would need to do, and why each piece is not a normalization/interpolation problem
but a separate, expensive inverse problem in its own right. Per the standing
instruction, this file is the deliverable; no interpolation was faked, no P-norm
numbers were computed for any orbit.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT P IS DEFINED ON (from archive/polar_spectrum.py + polar_cornerreg.py)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
z is the packed state of a STEADY, self-similar corner profile, found by Newton's
method (not a time series): three fields (A, B, P-field) plus two scalar gauge
unknowns (c_l, c_w), on a multi-panel Chebyshev grid in a self-similar radial
coordinate xi = ln(1+r) covering the FULL corner-to-outer domain
edges=(0.0, 2.0, 15.0, 25.0), crossed with an angular grid b in
[eps_b, pi/2-eps_b] (36 points, quarter-plane). For root A (the certified
production root): Nx=71 radial nodes, Nb=36 angular nodes, n2=2556,
N=3*n2+2=7670.

The fields A, B, P are DIVIDED variables: Om = xi*A, Bf = xi^2*B, Psi = xi^2*P,
further carrying an e^{a0 xi} self-similar exponential weight (a0 = alpha, the
solved-for blowup exponent). This is a nonlinear reparametrization of the
underlying vorticity/velocity/streamfunction triple, not a relabeling: recovering
A, B, P at a point requires already knowing xi, b, and a0 there.

Amplitude convention: the profile is gauge-fixed by two corner boundary values
(WX_REF=1.19620314, THXX_REF=1.79819132, i.e. A(0,b)=WX*cos(b),
B(0,b)=(THXX/2)*cos^2(b)) -- a FIXED corner normalization, not a sup-amplitude=1
normalization. z is explicitly documented as "UNNORMALIZED in amplitude" (recon
note, confirmed by re-reading polar_cornerreg.py __init__).

P itself (this run) lives on a further-reduced coordinate: the n_finite=4758-
dimensional Z0 (orthonormal SVD) basis of ker(Cg) inside the n_f=4760 "free"
coordinates of z (free = live transport rows, excluding axis pins and C0-duplicate
interface rows). P is an operator on Z0-basis coefficient vectors, not on z
directly and not on any physical quantity directly.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT THE ORBIT DATA IS (from features.py extract()/orbit())
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
U is built from actual time-dependent Dedalus DNS snapshots (HDF5, tasks
'omega1'/'u1' on the REAL physical (z, r) Eulerian grid, real simulation time t).
Per snapshot: compute vorticity magnitude mag = sqrt(wr^2+wt^2+wz^2) on the full
physical grid, find its peak (iz, ir), take a local box of up to 21x21=441 cells
CENTERED ON THE MOVING PEAK, sized by that snapshot's own HWHM length scale
Lp = sqrt(lz*lr), and normalize by A = sup|omega| at that snapshot (a
sup-amplitude=1, HWHM-length=1 convention -- unrelated to WX_REF/THXX_REF).
U therefore changes basis, origin, and scale from snapshot to snapshot, by
construction (features.py:133-140).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHY THE GAP IS NOT BRIDGEABLE CHEAPLY
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
A faithful map orbit-snapshot -> P-space coefficient vector would need ALL of:

1. LOCATE the self-similar blowup point (z*, t*) for that DNS run. Nothing in
   features.py or lambda_geom.py computes this; the peak (iz, ir) tracked per
   snapshot is a proxy, not a fitted blowup center, and no fitting code for it
   exists in this tree (grepped: no "blowup_time", "t_star", "self_similar" hits
   outside the solver files themselves).

2. INVERT the self-similar coordinate change (z, r, t) -> (xi, b) at that (z*, t*),
   which is the SOLVER's own definition (r = exp(xi)-1 is documented only as the
   solver's SEED construction, polar_cornerreg.py:120-121 -- there is no general
   physical-r -> xi map stated for arbitrary DNS output, and no code computes one).
   This also requires knowing which self-similar exponent a0 applies -- a0 is a
   Newton-solved property of the STEADY profile, not a measured property of a
   moving DNS snapshot.

3. RECOVER the three DECOUPLED fields (A, B, P-field) from a single scalar
   mag = |omega|: mag is sqrt(wr^2+wt^2+wz^2), a magnitude that already destroys
   the directional/component information the divided variables Om, Bf, Psi encode
   separately. There is no formula in this codebase (or invertible under it) that
   recovers Om, Bf, Psi, let alone A = Om/xi etc., from mag alone.

4. RENORMALIZE from features.py's per-snapshot sup=1, HWHM=1 local convention to
   the solver's FIXED corner-gauge (WX_REF, THXX_REF) global convention -- these are
   different normalizations of different objects (a local peak-relative crop vs. a
   globally gauge-fixed field over the whole domain), not a scalar rescale.

5. RESTRICT to the domain P is defined on: even after (1)-(4), the result would need
   to be evaluated over the FULL angular range b in [eps_b, pi/2-eps_b] and the FULL
   radial range xi in [0, 25.0] to be comparable to z -- but features.py's box is a
   LOCAL 21x21 crop around one moving point. A local crop cannot populate a global
   field without extrapolation, which is exactly the kind of faked interpolation
   this gate is here to refuse.

6. PROJECT into free/ker(Cg)/Z0 coordinates -- this step alone IS cheap and reuses
   real.restrict / real.project_oblique / kernel_basis as required, but it is the
   very last step and presupposes (1)-(5) already produced a length-N vector on the
   solver's own grid.

Each of (1)-(5) is a separate nontrivial inverse problem (blowup-point fitting,
coordinate un-warping, vector-field decomposition, gauge normalization, and
global-field reconstruction from a local sample) -- collectively comparable in cost
to re-deriving the self-similar profile from DNS data, which is a different, much
larger research program than this probe, not a "cheap hedge." No such code exists
in the tree today (grepped for pack/unpack/adopt_seed users, U_star/Ustar/fixed_point
references, and any DNS->spectral bridge: none found outside the solver's own seed
construction, which itself only maps a KNOWN ANALYTIC/interpolated seed shape, not
raw DNS output, onto the panel grid).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
VERDICT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
STOP at the feasibility gate, per instruction. No P-norm projection was attempted
on any orbit tag (available tags found under boussinesq/runs and repro/runs include
G5_11, N2_1e-3/1e-4, NUL1e-3/1e-4, OR_z128r384/z128r768/z256r384/z256r768, SYMa/SYMb,
W3R/W63R/W77R, loc1024, mpi1024 -- listed for completeness, not touched). No fake
interpolation was written. The cheap hedge does not exist as stated; closing this
gap is a separate, expensive project (a blowup-point/self-similar-fit pipeline from
DNS output into the corner solver's own coordinates), not a re-measurement of
existing data.

outcome=blocked
